"Generation Quality-Latency Tradeoff-Aware Inference Offloading for Multimodal LLMs in Cloud-Edge Continuum (https://arxiv.org/html/2606.15210v1)\nciteturn43734view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn43733view0\",\"lineno\":188}); Total lines: 496\nL136:  | $$\\mathcal{H}_{P_{u}}=\\text{DistilBERT}(\\mathcal{P}_{u},\\mathcal{Q}_{u})$$  |  | (6)\nL137: \nL138: where 768 is the default setting in the original paper of DistilBERT, and $L$ denotes the maximum length of the input text. Subsequently, the mean pooling in Eq. 【59†(7)】 is applied to $\\mathcal{H}_{text}$ to derive a fixed-length text embedding $f_{P_{u}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{768}$.\nL139: \nL140:  | $$f_{P_{u}}=\\frac{\\sum^{L}_{i=1}\\mathcal{Q}_{i}\\cdot\\mathcal{H}^{(i)}_{P_{u}}}{\\sum^{L}_{i=1}\\mathcal{Q}_{i}}$$  |  | (7)\nL141: For visual feature extraction, we utilize a frozen Vision Transformer[【60†25】] (i.e., vit-base-patch16-224^{9}^{9} 9 https://huggingface.co/google/vit-base-patch16-224) as our visual backbone to encode the image $I_{u}$ in the input prompt. The image is first divided into a set of non-overlapping patches, each of which is linearly projected to a 768-dimensional embedding.\nL142: Then we use the Vision Transformer model to extract the [CLS] token representation $f_{I_{u}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{768}$ as the global image feature through:\nL143:  | $$f_{I_{u}}=\\text{ViT}(I_{u})_{\\text{[CLS]}}$$  |  | (8)\nL144: ②Learnable feature projection modules: To align features from different modalities into a common latent space, we incorporate a learnable feature projection module following both the Vision Transformer and DistilBERT encoders. Each modality-specific feature (i.e., text feature $f_{P_{u}}$ and image feature $f_{I_{u}}$) is projected through a fully-connected layer with LayerNorm, Gaussian Error Linear Unit (GELU) activation, and Dropout regularization.\nL145: This process can be mathematically formulated as follows:\nL146:  | $\\displaystyle f^{\\prime}_{P_{u}}=\\text{Dropout}\\Big(\\text{GELU}\\big(\\text{LayerNorm}(W_{t_{1}}f_{P_{u}}+b_{t_{1}}\\big)\\big)\\Big)$  |  | (9)\nL147: \nL148: and\nL149: \nL150:  | $\\displaystyle f^{\\prime}_{I_{u}}=$  | $\\displaystyle\\text{Dropout}\\Big(\\text{GELU}\\big(\\text{LayerNorm}(W_{i_{1}}f_{I_{u}}+b_{i_{1}}\\big)\\big)\\Big)$  |  | (10)\nL151: where $f^{\\prime}_{P_{u}},f^{\\prime}_{I_{u}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{64}$ denote the projected features, and $W_{t_{1}},W_{i_{1}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{64\\times 768}$ are the projection weight matrices. This reduction in dimensionality serves as an information bottleneck that filters irrelevant features while preserving discriminative signals.\nL152: ③Categorical embedding layers for meta-information encoding: To incorporate meta-information about the inference environment, we encode the MLLM type $m_{x_{R_{u}}}$ and device type $d_{x_{R_{u}}}$ into $m^{\\prime}_{x_{R_{u}}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{32}$ and $d^{\\prime}_{x_{R_{u}}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{32}$ through two learnable embedding layers, respectively. This process can be formulated as follows:\nL153: \nL154:  | $$m^{\\prime}_{x_{R_{u}}}=\\text{Embedding}_{model}(m_{x_{R_{u}}})$$  |  | (11)\nL155: \nL156: and\nL157:  | $$d^{\\prime}_{x_{R_{u}}}=\\text{Embedding}_{device}(d_{x_{R_{u}}})$$  |  | (12)\nL158: \nL159: The categorical features are concatenated to form $f^{\\prime}_{{x_{R_{u}}}}=[m^{\\prime}_{x_{R_{u}}};d^{\\prime}_{x_{R_{u}}}]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{64}$, which represents systematic variations across different inference environment configurations.\nL160: ④A feature fusion layer for cross-modal integration: The projected features from all branches are concatenated and projected to a fused feature representation $f^{fused}_{x_{R_{u}}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{64}$ through a fusion network. This process can be formulated as follows:\nL161: \nL162:  | $$f^{fused}_{x_{R_{u}}}=\\text{Fusion}([f^{\\prime}_{P_{u}},f^{\\prime}_{I_{u}},f^{\\prime}_{x_{R_{u}}}])$$  |  | (13)\nL163: The fusion module $\\text{Fusion}(\\cdot)$ consists of two linear layers with LayerNorm, GELU activation, and Dropout regularization. This design enables effective cross-modal interaction while mitigating overfitting through aggressive regularization.\nL164: \nL165: Following the aforementioned neural network architecture, we can construct the MGQP and MILP modules using specific output heads. The details are as follows:\nL166: (1) The classification head for the MGQP module: For the task of MLLM generation quality prediction, we incorporate a classification head that maps the fused feature representation $f^{fused}_{x_{R_{u}}}$ to a discrete quality label $\\hat{b}_{x_{R_{u}}}\\in\\{0,1\\}$.\nL167: Specifically, a two-layer (i.e., $W^{\\text{log}}_{1}$ and $W^{\\text{log}}_{2}$) fully-connected layer with GELU activation projects $f^{fused}_{x_{R_{u}}}$ into logits $f^{\\text{log}}_{x_{R_{u}}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2}$ over 2 quality categories (i.e., failure and success). This process can be formulated as follows:\nL168:  | $$f^{\\text{log}}_{x_{R_{u}}}=W^{\\text{log}}_{2}\\cdot\\text{Dropout}\\Big(\\text{GELU}(W^{\\text{log}}_{1}f^{fused}_{x_{R_{u}}}+b^{\\text{log}}_{1}\\big)\\Big)+b^{\\text{log}}_{2}$$  |  | (14)\nL169: where $W^{\\text{log}}_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{32\\times 64}$ and $W^{\\text{log}}_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 32}$ are the weight matrices. During the training phase of the MGQP module, we use Focal Loss [【61†26】]. Focal Loss is extensively employed to mitigate the issue of class imbalance in classification tasks, with its weighting mechanism giving greater weight to examples from classes with small sample sizes. This loss function is defined as:\nL170:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{Focal}}(g_{x_{R_{u}}})=-\\alpha(1-p_{g_{x_{R_{u}}}})^{\\gamma}\\text{log}(p_{g_{x_{R_{u}}}})$$  |  | (15)\nL171: \nL172: where $\\alpha$ serves as a weight in the class proportion to balance the influence of different classes during training, $p_{g_{x_{R_{u}}}}$ is the model’s estimated probability for the “success” class, and $\\gamma$ is a hyperparameter to tune the sensitivity of the loss function to misclassified examples.\nL173: (2) The regression head for the MILP module: For the task of MLLM inference latency prediction, we incorporate a regression head that maps the fused feature representation $f^{fused}_{x_{R_{u}}}$ to a continuous latency label $\\hat{T}^{r}_{x_{R_{u}}}$. Specifically, a two-layer fully-connected layer with GELU activation projects $f^{fused}_{x_{R_{u}}}$ into a latency value $\\hat{T}^{r}_{x_{R_{u}}}\\in\\mathbb{R}$. This process can be mathematically formulated as follows:\nL174:  | $$\\hat{T}^{r}_{x_{R_{u}}}=W^{t}_{2}\\cdot\\text{GELU}(W^{t}_{1}f^{fused}_{x_{R_{u}}}+b^{t}_{1}\\big)+b^{t}_{2}$$  |  | (16)\nL175: where $W^{\\text{t}}_{1}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{32\\times 64}$ and $W^{\\text{t}}_{2}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times 32}$ are the weight matrices. During the training phase of the MGQP module, we use Huber Loss[【62†27】]. The Huber loss function serves as the cornerstone for robust regression training, effectively balancing the advantages of Mean Squared Error (MSE)[【63†28】] and Mean Absolute Error (MAE)[【64†29】] to mitigate the influence of outliers (i.e., timeout samples where ${T}^{r}_{x_{R_{u}}}>60\\text{s}$).\nL176: Formally, the Huber loss is defined as:\nL177:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{Huber}}(g_{x_{R_{u}}})=\\begin{cases}\\frac{1}{2}\\psi^{2},&\\text{if }|\\psi|\\leq\\delta,\\\\\nL178: \\delta|\\psi|-\\frac{1}{2}\\delta^{2},&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$  |  | (17)\nL179: \nL180: where $\\psi={T}^{r}_{x_{R_{u}}}-\\hat{T}^{r}_{x_{R_{u}}}$ represents the residual between the predicted latency $\\hat{T}^{r}_{x_{R_{u}}}$ and the ground truth ${T}^{r}_{x_{R_{u}}}$, and $\\delta$ is a hyperparameter dictating the transition point between MSE loss and MAE loss.\nL181: ### IV-B The Proposed Quality-Latency Tradeoff-Aware MLLM Inference Offloading (QLMIO) Framework\nL182: To address the complex multi-objective optimization problem $\\mathbf{P}$, we propose a QLMIO framework based on DRL. We use a 3-tuple $\\left(\\mathcal{S},\\mathcal{A},\\mathcal{R}\\right)$ to represent the Markov Decision Process (MDP) of the MLLM inference offloading, where $\\mathcal{S}=\\{s_{t}\\}_{t\\in T}$ is the state space, $\\mathcal{A}=\\{a_{t}\\}_{t\\in T}$ is the action space, $\\mathcal{R}=\\{r_{t}\\}_{t\\in T}$ is the reward set, and $T$ is the set of agent decision time slots.\nL183: The detailed definitions are as follows:\nL184: 【65†Image: Refer to caption】 Fig. 4: Developed architecture of the proposed QLMIO framework\nL185: \nL186: 1) State $s_{t}$: To comprehensively assess the completion quality of an MLLM inference task within the cloud-edge collaborative MLLM system, the state of the QLMIO in time slot $t$ is defined as follows:\nL187:  | $$\\begin{split}s_{t}=\\big(&P_{u},I_{u},m_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}},d_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}},\\\\\nL188: &\\hat{T}^{r}_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}},\\tilde{T}^{q}_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}},\\hat{b}_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}\\big)\\end{split}$$  |  | (18)\nL189: where $P_{u}$ and $I_{u}$ represent the text and image in the user prompt, respectively; $m_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}$ and $d_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}$ denote the MLLM type and the device type per server, respectively; $\\hat{T}^{r}_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}$ is the predicted MLLM inference latency per server using our designed MILP module; $\\tilde{T}^{q}_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}$ is the estimated queue load per server.\nL190: The estimated queue load $\\tilde{T}^{q}_{x_{R_{u}}}$ for one server $x_{R_{u}}$ is defined as the average predicted latency per queued task:\nL191:  | $$\\tilde{T}^{q}_{x_{R_{u}}}=\\hat{T}^{q}_{x_{R_{u}}}/|q_{x_{R_{u}}}|$$  |  | (19)\nL192: \nL193: where $\\hat{T}^{q}_{x_{R_{u}}}$ is the queue latency estimated by Eq. 【66†(3)】 and $|q_{x_{R_{u}}}|$ is the length of the task queue; $\\hat{b}_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}$ in the state denotes the generation correctness probabilities for the current task on each server predicted by our designed MGQP module.\nL194: 2) Action $a_{t}$: The proposed QLMIO framework determines the optimal server in $E$ or $C$ for MLLM inference offloading according to state information $s_{t}$. Therefore, we can formulate the offloading decision in time slot $t$ as the following discrete action:\nL195: \nL196:  | $$a_{t}\\in\\{E,C\\}$$  |  | (20)\nL197: 3) Reward $r_{t}$: Considering that the objective of problem $\\mathbf{P}$ is to minimize the average task offloading latency and the average task failure rate, while the goal of the DRL agent is to maximize the long-term reward, we formulate the immediate reward $r_{t}$ for performing the offloading action $a_{t}$ on task $R_{u}$ as follows:\nL198: \nL199:  | $$r_{t}=1-T_{{a_{t}}_{R_{u}}}/T_{\\text{Greedy}(R_{u})}+r^{b}_{t}$$  |  | (21)\nL200: where $T_{{a_{t}}_{R_{u}}}$ denotes the actual total offloading latency incurred after task $R_{u}$ is offloaded to server $a_{t}$, which is calculated by Eq. 【67†(2)】; $\\text{Greedy}(\\cdot)$ represents a greedy algorithm that offloads each task to the server with the shortest task queue, providing a total offloading latency $T_{\\text{Greedy}(\\cdot)}$ (according to Eq.\nL201: 【67†(2)】) for comparison; $r^{b}_{t}$ is the task completion reward, which equals $+1$ upon successful task completion and $-2$ in case of task failure, thereby incentivizing reliable inference delivery. Specifically, the primary rationale for setting the failure penalty to $-2$ is to maintain a balanced trade-off between the latency reward component (i.e., $1-T_{{a_{t}}_{R_{u}}}/T_{\\text{Greedy}(\\cdot)}$) and the task completion reward.\nL202: During the early exploration stages of training, the DRL agent may yield a decision latency $T_{{a_{t}}_{R_{u}}}$ that is several times larger than the greedy baseline $T_{\\text{Greedy}(\\cdot)}$, which severely amplifies the negative magnitude of the latency reward. Assigning a substantial penalty of $-2$ ensures that the requirement for task completion is not overwhelmed by extreme latency penalties, guiding the agent to jointly optimize both objectives.\nL203: Next, we need to determine an optimal offloading action in each decision time slot. Since the state space of the proposed QLMIO framework is continuous and the offloading action is discrete, we choose the Dueling Double Deep Q-Network (D3QN) [【68†30】] as the backbone. The core neural network architecture of QLMIO is shown in Fig. 【69†4】. Fig.\nL204: 【69†4】 shows: 1) The evaluation network of QLMIO framework employs the same multimodal feature extractor as the extractor contained in the MILP and MGQP modules to process text $P_{u}$ and image $I_{u}$ in the state $s_{t}$ (according to Eq.【70†(6)】 to Eq.【71†(10)】).\nL205: 2) Furthermore, QLMIO encodes the device types $d_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(\\mathcal{E}+1)\\cdot 16}$ and model types $m_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{(\\mathcal{E}+1)\\cdot 16}$ of all servers in state $s_{t}$ within the cloud-edge collaborative MLLM system through two embedding layers (according to Eq.【72†(11)】 and Eq. 【73†(12)】). $+1$ represents the cloud.\nL206: 3) After that, the features from all branches are concatenated and projected to a fused feature representation $f^{fused}_{\\{E,C\\}_{R_{u}}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{32}$ (according to Eq. 【74†(13)】), allowing unified representation of heterogeneous system components.\nL207: 4) Subsequently, the fused feature representation $f^{fused}_{\\{E,C\\}_{R_{u}}}$ is concatenated with both the inference latency $\\hat{T}^{r}_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{E}+1}$ predicted by MILP for task $R_{u}$ offloaded to each server and the queue load $\\tilde{T}^{q}_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{E}+1}$ estimated by Eq.\nL208: 【75†(19)】 for each server, along with the generation quality $\\hat{b}_{\\forall x_{R_{u}}\\in\\{E,C\\}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{E}+1}$ predicted by MGQP for task $R_{u}$ offloaded to each server, forming a combined representation $f_{R_{u}}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{32+3\\cdot(\\mathcal{E}+1)}$ that is then fed into a fully-connected layer dedicated to feature extraction. 5) This fully-connected layer consists of two linear layers with LayerNorm, GELU activation, and Dropout regularization.\nL209: The parameter matrices of the two layers correspond to the dimensions of $256\\times(32+(\\mathcal{E}+1)\\cdot 3)$ and $256\\times 256$, respectively. 6) Finally, QLMIO splits the network architecture into two branches to calculate the value function and the advantage function to learn both the state value and nuanced discrepancies between different actions.\nL210: Algorithm 1 The training process of the developed QLMIO framework\nL211: \nL212: Input: Set of users $U$, number of episodes $\\mathbb{E}$, set of edge servers $E$, cloud server $C$, training interval $\\mathbb{S}$, learning rate $\\eta$ and batch size $\\mathbb{B}$\nL213: \nL214: Output: Parameters of the evaluation network $\\theta_{en}$\nL215: \nL216: 1 Initialize a DRL model with an experience replay $\\mathbb{M}$, and the trained MILP and MGQP modules;\nL217: \nL218: 2 $step\\leftarrow 0$;\nL219: \nL220: 3 for $episode=1:\\mathbb{E}$ do\nL221:      4 $T^{q}\\leftarrow[0,\\dots,0]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{E}+1}$;\nL222: \nL223:      5 $T_{\\text{Greedy}(TQ_{U})}\\leftarrow[0,\\dots,0]\\in\\mathbb{R}^{\\mathcal{U}}$;\nL224: \nL225:      6 $t\\leftarrow 0,s_{t}\\leftarrow\\varnothing,TQ_{U}\\leftarrow\\varnothing$;\nL226: \nL227:      7 Each of $\\mathcal{U}$ users independently proposes an MLLM inference task within the environment and a task queue $TQ_{U}$ is constructed;\nL228: \nL229:      8 for $u=1:\\mathcal{U}$ do\nL230: \nL231:          9 $R_{u}\\leftarrow TQ_{U}\\left[u\\right]$;\nL232:          10 Obtain $T_{\\text{Greedy}(R_{u})}$ according to Eq. 【76†(21)】;\nL233: \nL234:          11 $T_{\\text{Greedy}(TQ_{U})}[u]\\leftarrow T_{\\text{Greedy}(R_{u})}$;\nL235: \nL236:      12 end for\nL237: \nL238:      13 for $u=1:\\mathcal{U}$ do\nL239: \nL240:          14 $R_{u}\\leftarrow TQ_{U}\\left[u\\right]$;\nL241: \nL242:          15 Generate state $s_{t}$ according to Eq. 【77†(18)】;\nL243: \nL244:          16 Select an action $a^{*}_{t}$ according to Eq. 【78†(23)】 and offload the task to server $a^{*}_{t}$;\nL245:          17 Retrieve the actual response latency $T^{r}_{{a^{*}_{t}}_{R_{u}}}$ and actual generation quality $b_{{a^{*}_{t}}_{R_{u}}}$ from the ground truth data (MIOBench dataset);\nL246: \nL247:          18 $T_{{a^{*}_{t}}_{R_{u}}}\\leftarrow T^{r}_{{a^{*}_{t}}_{R_{u}}}+T^{q}[a^{*}_{t}]$;\nL248: \nL249:          19 $T^{q}[a^{*}_{t}]\\leftarrow T^{r}_{{a^{*}_{t}}_{R_{u}}}+T^{q}[a^{*}_{t}]$;\nL250: \nL251:          20 Obtain $r^{b}_{t}$ in Eq. 【76†(21)】;\nL252:          21 $r_{t}\\leftarrow 1-T_{{a^{*}_{t}}_{R_{u}}}/T_{\\text{Greedy}(TQ_{U})}[u]+r^{b}_{t}$;\nL253: \nL254:          22 Observe state $s_{t+1}$ from environment;\nL255: \nL256:          23 Store $\\left(s_{t},a^{*}_{t},r_{t},s_{t+1}\\right)$ in $\\mathbb{M}$;\nL257: \nL258:          24 if $|\\mathbb{M}|>\\mathbb{B}$ & $step\\equiv 0\\pmod{\\mathbb{S}}$ then\nL259: \nL260:              25 Sample $\\mathbb{B}$ transitions from $\\mathbb{M}$;\nL261: \nL262:              26 Update $\\theta_{en}$ according to Eq. 【79†(17)】;\nL263: \nL264:          27 end if\nL265:          28 $s_{t}\\leftarrow s_{t+1},t\\leftarrow t+1,step\\leftarrow step+1$;\nL266: \nL267:      29 end for\nL268: \nL269:      30 $\\theta_{tn}\\leftarrow\\tau\\cdot\\theta_{en}+(1-\\tau)\\cdot\\theta_{tn}$;\nL270: \nL271:      31 //$\\tau$ is a hyperparameter used for soft update\nL272: \nL273: 32 end for\nL274: Now, we can present the complete training process for the proposed QLMIO framework in Algorithm 【80†1】. The DRL model of QLMIO is initialized (line 1) with its architecture depicted in Fig. 【69†4】.\nL275: Within each episode, the QLMIO framework initializes list $T^{q}$ (initialized to zeros and its length is equal to $\\mathcal{E}+1$) to record actual queuing latency for each server (line 4), and a list $T_{\\text{Greedy}(TQ_{U})}$ (initialized to zeros and its length is equal to $\\mathcal{U}$) to record the total offloading latency for generating the greedy algorithm decision for each task (line 5).\nL276: Subsequently, each of $\\mathcal{U}$ users independently proposes an MLLM inference task, and all tasks are aggregated into a collective task queue $TQ_{U}$ (lines 6-7). The QLMIO framework then offloads tasks using the greedy algorithm and populates $T_{\\text{Greedy}(TQ_{U})}$ with the resulting latencies (lines 8-12) for comparison.\nL277: After that, for each task in $TQ_{U}$ with state $s_{t}$, the QLMIO framework computes the value function $V_{\\theta_{en}}(s_{t})$ and the advantage function $A_{\\theta_{en}}(s_{t},a^{j}_{t})$ using the value layer and advantage layer, respectively, where $\\theta_{en}$ represents the parameters of the evaluation network.\nL278: Combining the value function $V_{\\theta_{en}}(s_{t})$ and the advantage function $A_{\\theta_{en}}(s_{t},a^{j}_{t})$ enables the Q function $Q_{\\theta_{en}}(s_{t},a^{j}_{t})$ to be calculated as follows:\nL279:  | $$Q_{\\theta_{en}}(s_{t},a^{j}_{t})=V_{\\theta_{en}}(s_{t})+A_{\\theta_{en}}(s_{t},a^{j}_{t})+\\frac{1}{\\mathcal{E}+1}\\sum_{i=1}^{\\mathcal{E}+1}A_{\\theta_{en}}(s_{t},a^{i}_{t})$$  |  | (22)\nL280: \nL281: where $a^{j}_{t}$ represents the action of offloading the task to the server $a^{j}_{t}\\in\\{E,C\\}$. Then, the QLMIO framework selects an action $a^{*}_{t}$ based on the $\\epsilon-greedy$ strategy [【81†31】] (lines 14-16). This action selection strategy is characterized as follows:\nL282:  | $$a^{*}_{t}=\\begin{cases}\\mathop{\\arg\\max}\\limits_{a^{j}_{t}}Q_{\\theta}(s_{t},a^{j}_{t}),&\\text{with probability }1-\\epsilon\\\\\nL283: \\text{random action},&\\text{with probability }\\epsilon\\end{cases}$$  |  | (23)\nL284: Since the MIOBench dataset we collected contains the actual response latency and generation quality resulting from offloading user tasks to a server in a real-world cloud-edge collaborative MLLM system, we can directly retrieve the actual response latency $T^{r}_{{a^{*}_{t}}_{R_{u}}}$ and generation quality $b_{{a^{*}_{t}}_{R_{u}}}$ from the MIOBench dataset (line 17).\nL285: After that, the total offloading latency $T_{{a^{*}_{t}}_{R_{u}}}$ and task completion reward $r^{b}_{t}$ can be calculated, from which the reward $r_{t}$ for executing action $a^{*}_{t}$ is derived (lines 18-21). Then, the QLMIO framework observes the next state $s_{t+1}$ and stores the transition $\\left(s_{t},a^{*}_{t},r_{t},s_{t+1}\\right)$ in the experience replay $\\mathbb{M}$ (lines 22-23).\nL286: When the size of experience replay $\\mathbb{M}$ exceeds the batch size $\\mathbb{B}$ and the number of decision steps exactly divides the training interval $\\mathbb{S}$, the QLMIO framework samples a batch of transitions from $\\mathbb{M}$ (lines 24-25). Subsequently, all parameters of the evaluation network are updated through backpropagation to minimize the Huber loss function (line 26).\nL287: Finally, the parameters of the evaluation network are transferred to the target network using soft update [【82†32】] at the end of each episode (line 30).\nL288: ## V Benchmark and Experiment\nL289: ### V-A An MLLM Inference Offloading Benchmark\nL290: \nL291: To assist the QLMIO framework in making optimal offloading decisions, it is imperative to train predictors for both MLLM inference latency and generation quality (i.e., the proposed MILP and MGQP modules). However, there are no public datasets tailored for the MLLM inference offloading problem. To fill this gap, we constructed a comprehensive MLLM inference offloading benchmark, MIOBench.\nL292: We selected the MMBench[【30†5】] dataset as the user task set. MMBench is a comprehensive multimodal dataset comprising 3,377 unique choice questions that integrate visual and textual elements. It encompasses 20 diverse MLLM inference task categories such as action recognition, future prediction, and spatial relation reasoning. Its broad scope effectively empowers a comprehensive and multi-dimensional evaluation of QLMIO.\nL293: Furthermore, we established a real-world cloud-edge collaborative MLLM system, with detailed configurations provided in Table 【83†I】. Architecturally, edge nodes are directly interconnected through the same-band Wi-Fi Local Area Network (LAN), while edge-to-cloud communication is provisioned over the cross-domain Wi-Fi Wide Area Network (WAN). After that, we deployed various MLLMs across servers of this cloud-edge system using Ollama^{10}^{10} 10 https://ollama.com/ according to Table 【83†I】.\nL294: TABLE I: Server configurations\nL295: \nL296:  | Device  | MLLM\nL297: Edge  | Jetson Orin Nano Super: 8GB  | Qwen3-VL:2B\nL298: RTX 3090Ti: 24GB  | Qwen3-VL:8B\nL299: Cloud  | RTX 5090Ti: 32GB  | Qwen3-VL:30B\nL300: Afterward, each task from MMBench is offloaded to every server to obtain the actual inference latency $T^{r}_{x_{R_{u}}}$ and generation quality $b_{x_{R_{u}}}$. These offloading traces constitute the MIOBench dataset. Specifically, each sample in MIOBench is formalized as a key-value pair, and the field descriptions are illustrated in Table 【84†II】.\nL301: TABLE II: Description of fields in our MIOBench dataset\nL302: Key  | Type  | Description\nL303: dataset  | String  | Source dataset name (i.e., MMBench).\nL304: prompt  | String  | Complete model input including context and options.\nL305: device_type  | String  | Hardware deployed for inference (e.g., RTX3090ti).\nL306: model_name  | String  | Name and scale of the evaluated MLLM (e.g., Qwen3-VL:8B).\nL307: score  | Integer  | generation quality (1: success, 0: failure, -1: timeout).\nL308: latency_ms  | Float  | Inference response latency in milliseconds.\nL309: sample_id  | Integer  | Unique identifier for the specific sample.\nL310: index  | Integer  | Sequential row index within the dataset.\nL311: source  | String  | Original task source (e.g., YouTube^{11}^{11} 11 https://news.youtube.com).\nL312: Ultimately, we compiled 10,131 MLLM inference offloading records from 3,377 diverse tasks, which constitute the MIOBench dataset. This dataset can be accessed from GitHub^{12}^{12} 12 https://anonymous.4open.science/r/MIOBench.\nL313: ### V-B Developed CEMLLM-Sim and Experiment Settings\nL314: Since the actual inference response latency and generation quality resulting from offloading each task to any server in our cloud-edge collaborative MLLM system are already recorded in MIOBench, we developed a Cloud-Edge Collaborative MLLM System simulator (CEMLLM-Sim), dedicated to training and evaluating MLLM offloading algorithms, and to facilitating the research on the related research in the community.\nL315: Within this simulator, we obtain the exact latency and quality metrics for any offloading decision by retrieving MIOBench, thereby avoiding the exorbitant costs associated with building a large-scale cloud-edge collaborative system. In all experiments presented in this paper, MIOBench was split into training, validation, and test sets at a ratio of 8:1:1 to thoroughly evaluate algorithm performance. Additionally, CEMLLM-Sim supports user-defined system configurations.\nL316: We train and evaluate our algorithms primarily in three different cloud-edge MLLM system configurations, as summarized in Table 【85†III】.\nL317: TABLE III: System configurations\nL318: Device  | MLLM  | Role  | Server Count\nL319: 5  | 10  | 15\nL320: RTX 5090  | Qwen3-VL:30B  | Cloud  | 1  | 1  | 1\nL321: RTX 3090Ti  | Qwen3-VL:8B  | Edge  | 1  | 2  | 4\nL322: Jetson Orin Nano Super  | Qwen3-VL:2B  | Edge  | 3  | 7  | 10\n"
